\paperw12240\paperh15840\margl1440\margr1440\margt1440\margb1440
\fs24
\pard\qc\sb120\sa200{\b\fs36 Comparative Evaluation of Ensemble and Tree-Based Models for Compressive Strength of Recycled Aggregate Concrete}\par
\pard\qc\sa120\sl276\slmult1 {\fs24 Author One\u185?, Author Two\u178?, Author Three\u179?}\par
\pard\qc\sa120\sl276\slmult1 {\fs20 \i \u185? Department of Civil Engineering, \u178? School of Civil Engineering, \u179? Department of Structural Engineering}\par
\pard\qc\sa120\sl276\slmult1 {\fs20 \i Corresponding author: author.one@example.edu}\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 {\b Abstract  }{\fs22 The growing use of recycled coarse aggregate (RCA) in structural concrete is constrained by the difficulty of predicting its compressive strength, which is governed by many interacting mix-design and material variables. This study develops and compares eight data-driven models, three single tree-based learners (classification and regression tree, CART; reduced-error pruning tree, REPTree; and the M5P model tree) and five ensemble learners (adaptive boosting, AdaBoost.R2; extremely randomised trees, Extra Trees; random forest, RF; gradient boosted regression trees, GBRT; and extreme gradient boosting, XGBoost), for estimating the 28-day and age-dependent compressive strength of recycled aggregate concrete. A database of 514 experimental records with ten input variables was assembled, cleaned, normalised, and split into 70% training and 30% testing subsets. Model quality was quantified with the correlation coefficient (R), coefficient of determination (R\u178?), mean absolute error (MAE), root mean square error (RMSE), mean absolute percentage error (MAPE), and Nash\u8211?Sutcliffe efficiency (NSE). The results show that ensemble learners consistently outperform single trees. XGBoost gave the best generalisation, with a testing R\u178? of 0.962, RMSE of 3.41 MPa, and MAE of 2.58 MPa, followed closely by GBRT and RF, whereas the single CART model was the weakest (testing R\u178? of 0.861). A SHAP-based interpretation identified curing age, the water-to-cement ratio, cement content, and the RCA replacement ratio as the most influential predictors. The findings confirm that gradient-boosted ensembles provide an accurate, robust, and interpretable tool for the mix design of sustainable recycled aggregate concrete.}\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 {\b Keywords  }{\fs22 Recycled aggregate concrete \u183? Compressive strength \u183? Ensemble learning \u183? Gradient boosting \u183? Random forest \u183? Decision tree \u183? Machine learning \u183? SHAP analysis}\par
\pard\sb320\sa120{\b\fs28 1  Introduction}\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 The construction sector is among the largest consumers of natural resources and a major source of anthropogenic carbon dioxide, with concrete alone accounting for a substantial share of global material consumption [1]. The twin pressures of dwindling natural aggregate reserves and the escalating volume of construction and demolition (C&D) waste have made the recycling of crushed concrete into recycled coarse aggregate (RCA) an increasingly attractive route toward a circular construction economy [2]. Replacing natural coarse aggregate (NCA) with RCA reduces landfill demand, conserves virgin quarried stone, and lowers the embodied energy of new concrete [3]. Despite these environmental advantages, the structural adoption of recycled aggregate concrete (RAC) remains cautious because its mechanical behaviour is more variable and generally inferior to that of conventional concrete produced with natural aggregate [4].\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 The principal reason for this variability is the residual mortar that adheres to recycled aggregate particles. The old adhered mortar is porous, micro-cracked, and heterogeneous, so it raises water absorption, lowers the particle density, and introduces a second, weaker interfacial transition zone between the old and new cement pastes [5]. As a consequence, the compressive strength of RAC depends not only on the usual parameters of plain concrete, such as cement content, water-to-cement ratio, and curing age, but also on the replacement ratio, the water absorption and density of the recycled aggregate, and the effectiveness of any pre-treatment or mixing procedure [6]. These many interacting factors produce a strongly non-linear relationship between mix composition and strength that classical regression formulae, calibrated for ordinary concrete, cannot capture reliably [7].\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Compressive strength is the single most important mechanical property used in structural design and quality control, and it is conventionally determined by casting and crushing standard specimens after 28 days of curing [8]. This experimental route is accurate but slow, labour-intensive, and material-consuming; it also provides little guidance for optimising a mix before the specimens are cast. For recycled aggregate concrete, where the design space is large and the sources of raw material are diverse, the cost of exhaustive trial batching becomes prohibitive [9]. There is therefore a clear need for fast, reliable, and inexpensive predictive tools that can estimate compressive strength directly from mix composition and material properties [10].\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Over the past decade, machine learning (ML) has emerged as a powerful alternative to empirical and semi-empirical equations for modelling the properties of cementitious materials [11]. Unlike fixed-form regression, ML algorithms learn the underlying mapping between inputs and outputs directly from data, without assuming a particular functional shape, and they handle high-dimensional, non-linear, and interacting variables naturally [12]. Artificial neural networks were among the earliest techniques applied to concrete strength prediction, and they have been shown to reproduce experimental trends with high accuracy [13]. Support vector machines, adaptive neuro-fuzzy inference systems, and gene expression programming have likewise been used to model the strength and durability of both conventional and recycled aggregate concrete [14]. These studies collectively demonstrate that soft-computing models can substantially reduce the experimental effort associated with mix design [15].\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Among the many ML families, tree-based methods occupy a special place because they are interpretable, insensitive to the scaling of inputs, and able to capture sharp, threshold-type effects that are common in concrete behaviour [16]. A single decision tree, however, is a high-variance learner: small perturbations in the training data can change its structure dramatically, and an unpruned tree tends to overfit [17]. Ensemble learning overcomes this weakness by combining many base trees into a single, more stable predictor. Two broad strategies dominate: bagging, which trains base learners on bootstrap replicates and averages their outputs to reduce variance, and boosting, which builds learners sequentially so that each new member focuses on the residual errors of its predecessors to reduce bias [18]. Random forest, a refinement of bagging with additional feature randomisation, and the gradient-boosting family, including the highly optimised XGBoost implementation, are now regarded as state-of-the-art for tabular regression tasks [19].\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Although individual ML models have frequently been applied to recycled aggregate concrete, the literature still lacks a systematic, like-for-like comparison of single tree-based learners against modern ensemble learners on a common database, using an identical pre-processing pipeline and a consistent set of evaluation metrics [20]. Many previous studies report only one or two algorithms, use different datasets, or omit an interpretability analysis that would let engineers understand which variables drive the prediction. The present work addresses this gap. It assembles a unified database of recycled aggregate concrete mixes, trains three single trees and five ensemble learners under identical conditions, ranks them with six complementary error measures, and interprets the best model with a game-theoretic feature-attribution method.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Design codes and national standards have historically limited the proportion of recycled aggregate permitted in structural concrete, often to conservative upper bounds, precisely because the available predictive tools could not guarantee a target strength with sufficient confidence. This caution, although prudent, slows the diffusion of a material that could divert enormous quantities of demolition waste from landfill. A predictive model that is both accurate and transparent therefore has value beyond the laboratory: it can underpin performance-based specifications in which a mix is accepted on the strength it is predicted and later verified to deliver, rather than on prescriptive composition limits alone. The development of such models is one of the practical motivations for the present comparison.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 The scatter observed in reported recycled aggregate concrete strengths arises from several superimposed sources of heterogeneity. The parent concrete from which the aggregate is crushed varies in original strength, age, and exposure history; the crushing and sieving process controls the amount of adhered mortar and the particle-size distribution; and the degree of saturation of the porous aggregate at mixing strongly affects the effective water available for hydration. These factors interact with the conventional mix variables in ways that are difficult to express with a closed-form equation but that a flexible, data-driven model can learn if it is exposed to a sufficiently rich and varied database. Capturing these interactions, rather than merely fitting a smooth trend, is where modern ensemble learners are expected to excel.\par
\pard\sb320\sa120{\b\fs28 2  Research Significance}\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 This study provides a transparent and reproducible benchmark that quantifies exactly how much predictive accuracy is gained by moving from a single decision tree to bagged and boosted ensembles for recycled aggregate concrete. By holding the database, the train/test split, and the performance metrics constant across all eight algorithms, the comparison isolates the contribution of the learning strategy itself. The resulting ranking helps practitioners choose an appropriate model given their accuracy, interpretability, and computational constraints. In addition, the feature-importance analysis translates a black-box prediction into actionable mix-design guidance, indicating which variables should be controlled most tightly to achieve a target strength. Collectively, the work supports the wider and more confident use of recycled aggregate in structural concrete, contributing to resource conservation and waste reduction.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 From a methodological standpoint, the study also contributes a reusable template for the fair evaluation of competing learners in civil-engineering regression problems. By specifying the data cleaning, the normalisation, the partitioning, the cross-validated tuning, and the reporting of a balanced set of error measures, it reduces the risk of the optimistic, single-metric claims that sometimes accompany the introduction of a new algorithm. The framework can be applied without modification to other target properties, such as tensile strength, elastic modulus, or durability indices, and to other sustainable concretes that incorporate supplementary cementitious materials or alternative aggregates.\par
\pard\sb320\sa120{\b\fs28 3  Methodology Overview}\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 The overall research workflow is summarised in Fig. 1. It begins with the compilation of an experimental database from the published literature, followed by data screening, cleaning, and normalisation. The cleaned data are divided into training and testing subsets; the training subset is used to fit each model and to tune its hyper-parameters by cross-validation, while the testing subset, unseen during training, is reserved for an unbiased assessment of generalisation. Eight algorithms are developed in parallel, their predictions are evaluated with a common suite of statistical indicators, and the best-performing model is finally subjected to a SHAP-based sensitivity analysis.\par
\pard\qc\sa120\sl276\slmult1 {\pict\pngblip\picwgoal8640\pichgoal6203
}\par
\pard\qc\sa120\sl276\slmult1 {\fs20 \b Fig. 1  }{\fs20 Overall workflow adopted for the development, tuning, and comparison of the eight tree-based and ensemble models.}\par
\pard\sb320\sa120{\b\fs28 4  Machine Learning Models}\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 This section describes the eight algorithms considered in the study. For a regression problem with N training examples, each example i is characterised by a feature vector x\u7522? containing the ten mix-design variables and by a measured compressive strength y\u7522?. A model produces an estimate \u375?\u7522? of the strength, and learning consists of choosing the model parameters that minimise a suitable loss between the measured and predicted values.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100{\b\fs26 4.1  Classification and Regression Tree (CART)}\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 A regression tree recursively partitions the feature space into disjoint regions and fits a constant to each region. At every node, the split that most reduces the impurity of the response is selected. For regression, impurity is measured by the standard deviation of the target, and the quality of a split is expressed as the standard deviation reduction (SDR) [21]:\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab SDR = sd(T) \u8722? \u8721?{\sub i} (|T{\sub i}|)/(|T|)\u183?sd(T{\sub i})\tab (1)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 where T is the set of examples reaching the node, T\u7522? are the subsets produced by a candidate split, and |\u183?| denotes set cardinality. The standard deviation of a set of n responses is\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab sd(T) = \u8730?((1)/(n)\u8721?{\sub i=1}{\super n} (y{\sub i} \u8722? y\u772?){\super 2})\tab (2)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 in which \u563? is the mean response of the node. Splitting continues until a stopping criterion is met, after which the value assigned to each terminal region R\u8344? is the average of the responses it contains:\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab c{\sub m} = (1)/(|R{\sub m}|)\u8721?{\sub x{\sub i}\u8712?R{\sub m}} y{\sub i}\tab (3)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Equivalently, the tree-growing algorithm chooses, at each step, the splitting variable j and cut-point s that minimise the total squared error of the two child regions:\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab \u8721?{\sub x{\sub i}\u8712?R{\sub 1}} (y{\sub i} \u8722? c{\sub 1}){\super 2} + \u8721?{\sub x{\sub i}\u8712?R{\sub 2}} (y{\sub i} \u8722? c{\sub 2}){\super 2}\tab (4)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Because a fully grown tree overfits, cost-complexity pruning is applied to trade tree size against accuracy, producing the compact CART model used as the simplest baseline in this study.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Despite its transparency, a single CART model is an unstable, high-variance learner. Because the partition is built greedily, a small change in the training data can alter an early split and propagate through the whole tree, producing a markedly different structure and different predictions. The piecewise-constant surface it generates is also inherently coarse: within each terminal region every mix receives the same predicted strength, so smooth gradients in the response, such as the gradual gain of strength with age, are approximated by a staircase. These properties make CART an informative lower bound against which the benefit of aggregation can be measured, rather than a competitive predictor in its own right.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100{\b\fs26 4.2  M5P Model Tree}\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 The M5P model tree, introduced by Quinlan and later refined, generalises the regression tree by placing a multivariate linear model, rather than a constant, at each leaf [22]. The tree is first grown using the standard deviation reduction criterion of Eq. (1), and a linear regression is then fitted to the examples reaching each leaf:\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab y = w{\sub 0} + w{\sub 1}x{\sub 1} + w{\sub 2}x{\sub 2} + \u8943? + w{\sub k}x{\sub k}\tab (5)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 where w\u8320? is the intercept and w\u8321? to w\u8342? are the regression coefficients of the k attributes retained at the leaf. To avoid sharp discontinuities between adjacent leaves, the prediction is smoothed by combining the leaf estimate with the models along the path to the root:\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab p{\super '} = (np + kq)/(n + k)\tab (6)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 in which p is the prediction passed up from the child node, q is the value predicted by the linear model at the current node, n is the number of training cases reaching the child, and k is a smoothing constant. The smoothed prediction p\u8242? improves accuracy, particularly when the leaf models differ markedly.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Because each leaf carries a linear model rather than a single constant, M5P usually needs far fewer leaves than CART to reach comparable accuracy, and it reproduces smooth trends more faithfully. The method therefore combines much of the interpretability of a tree with the local precision of linear regression, and the explicit leaf equations are attractive to engineers who want to understand, and if necessary audit, how a prediction is formed. Its main weakness is that it remains a single model and so still carries appreciable variance compared with an ensemble.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100{\b\fs26 4.3  Reduced-Error Pruning Tree (REPTree)}\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 REPTree is a fast regression-tree learner that builds a tree using the same variance-reduction logic as CART and then simplifies it by reduced-error pruning [23]. During pruning, the algorithm sets aside part of the training data as a pruning set and repeatedly removes the sub-tree whose elimination least increases the error on that set, replacing it with a leaf. This bottom-up procedure yields a small, low-variance tree quickly and provides a second single-tree baseline whose behaviour can be contrasted with that of CART and M5P.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 REPTree is valued chiefly for its speed and simplicity: the variance-based splitting and the reduced-error pruning are inexpensive, so the model trains quickly even on larger datasets. It typically yields a slightly smaller and more stable tree than an unpruned CART, but it shares the fundamental limitation of all single trees, namely high sensitivity to the particular sample used for training. In this study it serves, alongside CART and M5P, to characterise the performance that a practitioner could expect from a stand-alone tree before the benefits of ensembling are introduced.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100{\b\fs26 4.4  Bagging and Random Forest}\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Bagging, or bootstrap aggregation, reduces the variance of an unstable learner by training B independent base models on bootstrap resamples of the training data and averaging their outputs [24]:\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab y\u772?{\sub bag} = (1)/(B)\u8721?{\sub b=1}{\super B} h{\sub b}(x)\tab (7)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 where h_b is the b-th base tree. If the individual trees each have variance \u963?\u178? and an average pairwise correlation \u961?, the variance of the averaged prediction is\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab Var(y\u772?{\sub bag}) = \u961?\u963?{\super 2} + (1 \u8722? \u961?)/(B)\u963?{\super 2}\tab (8)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Equation (8) shows that averaging drives the second term to zero as B grows, but the first term persists unless the correlation between trees is reduced. Random forest achieves this de-correlation by selecting the best split at each node from a random subset of the features rather than from all of them [25]. The forest prediction is the average over B randomised trees, each grown on a bootstrap sample with a random parameter set \u920?_b:\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab y\u772?{\sub RF} = (1)/(B)\u8721?{\sub b=1}{\super B} T(x; \u920?{\sub b})\tab (9)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 A useful by-product of bootstrap sampling is the out-of-bag (OOB) estimate of error, computed for each example from only those trees that did not use it in training:\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab err{\sub OOB} = (1)/(N)\u8721?{\sub i=1}{\super N} (y{\sub i} \u8722? y\u772?{\sub i}{\super OOB}){\super 2}\tab (10)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 The OOB error provides an internal, nearly unbiased validation measure without a separate hold-out set, and it is also used to rank the importance of the input variables.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Random forest inherits the scale-invariance and the ability to model interactions that make trees attractive, while largely removing their instability. Because the trees are grown independently, training is readily parallelised, and the method is comparatively insensitive to its hyper-parameters: provided enough trees are grown, performance tends to plateau rather than degrade, which makes the model easy to deploy in practice. Its predictions are, however, less interpretable than those of a single tree, and because it averages rather than extrapolates, it cannot predict strengths outside the range spanned by the training data.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100{\b\fs26 4.5  Extremely Randomised Trees (Extra Trees)}\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Extra Trees push the randomisation of the forest one step further [26]. Instead of searching for the locally optimal cut-point of each candidate feature, the algorithm draws a cut-point at random for each feature and then keeps the feature whose random split maximises a normalised variance-reduction score:\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab Score(s, T) = (Var(T) \u8722? (|T{\sub L}|)/(|T|)Var(T{\sub L}) \u8722? (|T{\sub R}|)/(|T|)Var(T{\sub R}))/(Var(T))\tab (11)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 where T_L and T_R are the left and right child sets of split s. The additional randomness, together with the use of the whole training set rather than bootstrap resamples, lowers variance further and reduces training time, usually at the cost of a small increase in bias.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 The additional randomisation of Extra Trees can be a double-edged sword. By choosing cut-points at random it reduces variance and trains even faster than a random forest, which is advantageous on large, noisy datasets; but the same randomness can raise bias if the signal is concentrated in a few sharp thresholds, as is sometimes the case with curing age. The net effect on accuracy therefore depends on the problem, and comparing Extra Trees with random forest on the same data reveals whether the extra randomisation helps or hurts for recycled aggregate concrete.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100{\b\fs26 4.6  Adaptive Boosting (AdaBoost.R2)}\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 AdaBoost.R2 is the regression variant of adaptive boosting [27]. It trains a sequence of weak trees on reweighted versions of the data, giving larger weights to the examples that are currently predicted poorly. At iteration t, each example receives a loss derived from its absolute error, scaled by the largest error in the current set:\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab L{\sub i} = (|y{\sub i} \u8722? h{\sub t}(x{\sub i})|)/(D),   D = max{\sub i} |y{\sub i} \u8722? h{\sub t}(x{\sub i})|\tab (12)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 The weighted average loss of the current learner is then\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab L{\sub t}\u772? = \u8721?{\sub i=1}{\super N} w{\sub i}L{\sub i}\tab (13)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 and its confidence in the final committee is governed by\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab \u946?{\sub t} = (L{\sub t}\u772?)/(1 \u8722? L{\sub t}\u772?)\tab (14)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 A low average loss gives a small \u946? and hence a high weight. The example weights are updated so that difficult cases receive more attention in the next round:\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab w{\sub i}{\super new} = w{\sub i}\u946?{\sub t}{\super 1 \u8722? L{\sub i}}\tab (15)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 After T rounds, the ensemble output is the weighted combination of the base learners, where the weights are the log-confidences ln(1/\u946?_t):\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab y\u772? = (\u8721?{\sub t=1}{\super T} ln((1)/(\u946?{\sub t}))h{\sub t}(x))/(\u8721?{\sub t=1}{\super T} ln((1)/(\u946?{\sub t})))\tab (16)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 AdaBoost.R2 is conceptually elegant but can be sensitive to noisy records and outliers, because the reweighting scheme forces later learners to concentrate on the hardest, and sometimes mislabelled, cases. In an experimental concrete database, where measurement scatter is unavoidable, this behaviour may cap the accuracy that adaptive boosting can reach. It nevertheless represents an important historical and conceptual bridge between simple averaging and the gradient-based boosting that follows, and its performance here helps to separate the contribution of the boosting idea from that of its modern, regularised refinements.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100{\b\fs26 4.7  Gradient Boosted Regression Trees (GBRT)}\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Gradient boosting constructs the ensemble as a forward, stage-wise additive expansion in which each new tree is fitted to the negative gradient of the loss function evaluated at the current model [28]. The model after m stages is\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab F{\sub m}(x) = F{\sub m\u8722?1}(x) + \u957? \u947?{\sub m}h{\sub m}(x)\tab (17)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 where \u957? is a learning rate (shrinkage) in the interval (0, 1], h_m is the m-th regression tree, and \u947?_m is its optimal multiplier. Training minimises the total loss over the training set:\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab \u8466? = \u8721?{\sub i=1}{\super N} L(y{\sub i}, F(x{\sub i}))\tab (18)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 At each stage the pseudo-residuals, i.e. the negative gradients of the loss with respect to the current predictions, are computed for every example:\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab r{\sub im} = \u8722? [(\u8706?L(y{\sub i}, F(x{\sub i})))/(\u8706?F(x{\sub i}))]{\sub F = F{\sub m\u8722?1}}\tab (19)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 For the squared-error loss adopted here the pseudo-residuals reduce to the ordinary residuals, because\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab L(y, F) = (1)/(2)(y \u8722? F){\super 2}  \u8658?  r{\sub im} = y{\sub i} \u8722? F{\sub m\u8722?1}(x{\sub i})\tab (20)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 A new tree is then fitted to these residuals, and the step size for each terminal region is obtained by a one-dimensional line search:\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab \u947?{\sub m} = arg min{\sub \u947?} \u8721?{\sub i=1}{\super N} L(y{\sub i}, F{\sub m\u8722?1}(x{\sub i}) + \u947? h{\sub m}(x{\sub i}))\tab (21)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 The expansion is initialised with the constant that minimises the loss, which for squared error is simply the mean of the training targets:\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab F{\sub 0}(x) = arg min{\sub \u947?} \u8721?{\sub i=1}{\super N} L(y{\sub i}, \u947?) = y\u772?\tab (22)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 The three hyper-parameters that most affect gradient boosting are the number of trees, the tree depth, and the learning rate, and they interact strongly: a smaller learning rate generally improves accuracy but demands more trees, while deeper trees capture higher-order interactions at the cost of a greater risk of overfitting. Shrinkage and subsampling act as regularisers that temper this risk, and careful cross-validated tuning of these quantities is essential to realise the full potential of the method. When well tuned, gradient boosting fits complex response surfaces with low bias while keeping variance in check.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100{\b\fs26 4.8  Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost)}\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 XGBoost is a scalable and heavily regularised implementation of gradient boosting that has achieved outstanding results on a wide range of tabular problems [29]. Its distinguishing feature is a regularised objective that penalises model complexity explicitly. At boosting round t the objective is\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab Obj{\super (t)} = \u8721?{\sub i=1}{\super N} l(y{\sub i}, y\u772?{\sub i}{\super (t\u8722?1)} + f{\sub t}(x{\sub i})) + \u937?(f{\sub t})\tab (23)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 where the regularisation term for a tree with T leaves and leaf weights w_j is\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab \u937?(f) = \u947? T + (1)/(2)\u955? \u8721?{\sub j=1}{\super T} w{\sub j}{\super 2}\tab (24)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 with \u947? penalising the number of leaves and \u955? penalising their magnitude. Expanding the loss to second order with a Taylor series gives a tractable surrogate objective:\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab Obj{\super (t)} \u8771? \u8721?{\sub i=1}{\super N} (g{\sub i}f{\sub t}(x{\sub i}) + (1)/(2)h{\sub i}f{\sub t}{\super 2}(x{\sub i})) + \u937?(f{\sub t})\tab (25)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 in which g_i and h_i are the first- and second-order gradients of the loss with respect to the previous prediction:\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab g{\sub i} = (\u8706? l)/(\u8706?y\u772?{\sub i}{\super (t\u8722?1)}),   h{\sub i} = (\u8706?{\super 2} l)/(\u8706?y\u772?{\sub i}{\super 2(t\u8722?1)})\tab (26)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 For a fixed tree structure, the objective is minimised in closed form. The optimal weight of leaf j, with instance set I_j, G_j = \u931? g_i and H_j = \u931? h_i, is\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab w{\sub j}{\super *} = \u8722? (G{\sub j})/(H{\sub j} + \u955?)\tab (27)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 and substituting it back gives the structure score that measures the quality of the tree:\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab Obj{\super *} = \u8722? (1)/(2)\u8721?{\sub j=1}{\super T} (G{\sub j}{\super 2})/(H{\sub j} + \u955?) + \u947? T\tab (28)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Candidate splits are evaluated with the gain they produce, which balances the improvement in the structure score against the complexity penalty of adding a leaf:\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab Gain = (1)/(2)((G{\sub L}{\super 2})/(H{\sub L} + \u955?) + (G{\sub R}{\super 2})/(H{\sub R} + \u955?) \u8722? ((G{\sub L} + G{\sub R}){\super 2})/(H{\sub L} + H{\sub R} + \u955?)) \u8722? \u947?\tab (29)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Together with built-in shrinkage, column subsampling, and sparsity-aware split finding, this formulation makes XGBoost both accurate and resistant to overfitting [30], which motivates its inclusion as the most advanced ensemble in the comparison.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Beyond raw accuracy, XGBoost offers several practical advantages that recommend it for engineering use. It handles missing values natively through its sparsity-aware split finding, scales efficiently to large datasets through cache-aware and out-of-core computation, and exposes a rich set of regularisation controls that let the analyst trade complexity against generalisation in a principled way. Combined with widely available and well-documented software implementations, these features make it a convenient default for tabular regression problems such as the one studied here.\par
\pard\sb320\sa120{\b\fs28 5  Database Compilation and Preprocessing}\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 A database of 514 compressive-strength records for recycled aggregate concrete was assembled from peer-reviewed experimental studies [31]. Each record is described by ten input variables that collectively define the mix and the quality of the recycled aggregate, together with the measured compressive strength as the single output. The inputs are the cement content (C), the water content (W), the fine-aggregate content (FA), the natural coarse-aggregate content (NCA), the recycled coarse-aggregate content (RCA), the water absorption of the recycled aggregate (WA), the recycled-aggregate replacement ratio (RR), the superplasticiser dosage (SP), the water-to-cement ratio (w/c), and the curing age in days. The statistical description of all variables is given in Table 1.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 {\fs20 \b Table 1  }{\fs20 Statistical description of the input variables and the output (compressive strength) in the compiled database (n = 514).}\par
\pard
