\pard\sa120
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 The wide ranges in Table 1 reflect the diversity of the source studies and confirm that the database spans the practical design space of recycled aggregate concrete, from low-strength mixes with full replacement and high water absorption to high-strength mixes with natural aggregate only. The linear dependence among the variables was examined through the Pearson correlation matrix shown in Fig. 2. Curing age and cement content correlate positively with strength, whereas the water-to-cement ratio, the replacement ratio, and the water absorption of the recycled aggregate correlate negatively, in agreement with established concrete mechanics [32]. No pair of inputs exhibits a correlation strong enough to indicate harmful multicollinearity, so all ten variables were retained.\par
\pard\qc\sa120\sl276\slmult1 {\pict\pngblip\picwgoal8640\pichgoal7866
}\par
\pard\qc\sa120\sl276\slmult1 {\fs20 \b Fig. 2  }{\fs20 Pearson correlation matrix for the ten predictors and the compressive strength; warm colours denote positive and cool colours negative correlations.}\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 The distribution of the measured compressive strength and of the recycled-aggregate replacement ratio is shown in Fig. 3. The strength histogram is approximately bell-shaped and centred near 42 MPa, while the replacement ratio is well populated at the technologically important levels of 0, 25, 50, 75, and 100%. This balance ensures that the models are exposed to the full spectrum of recycled-aggregate usage rather than to a narrow band.\par
\pard\qc\sa120\sl276\slmult1 {\pict\pngblip\picwgoal8640\pichgoal4652
}\par
\pard\qc\sa120\sl276\slmult1 {\fs20 \b Fig. 3  }{\fs20 Distribution of the database: (a) histogram of measured compressive strength; (b) number of records at each recycled-aggregate replacement level.}\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Because the inputs differ by several orders of magnitude, each variable was linearly scaled to the interval [0, 1] by min-max normalisation before training:\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab x{\sub norm} = (x \u8722? x{\sub min})/(x{\sub max} \u8722? x{\sub min})\tab (30)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 where x is the raw value and x_min and x_max are the minimum and maximum of that variable in the training set. Normalisation places all inputs on a comparable footing, speeds up convergence, and prevents variables with large numerical ranges from dominating the distance-based and gradient-based computations [33]. The normalised data were then partitioned at random into a training subset containing 70% of the records (360 mixes) and a testing subset containing the remaining 30% (154 mixes); the testing subset was withheld entirely during model fitting and hyper-parameter tuning.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 The random partition was repeated with a fixed seed so that every model saw exactly the same training and testing records, ensuring that differences in performance can be attributed to the algorithms rather than to the split. The statistical profile of the training subset was verified to match that of the full database, so that the testing subset constitutes a fair and representative hold-out. During tuning, the training subset was further divided internally by ten-fold cross-validation, in which the data are split into ten parts and each part serves once as a validation fold while the remaining nine are used for fitting; the average validation error over the ten folds guided the selection of hyper-parameters and guarded against tuning to a single lucky split.\par
\pard\sb320\sa120{\b\fs28 6  Performance Evaluation Metrics}\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Six complementary indicators were used to quantify model quality, computed separately for the training and testing subsets. In the following, y\u7522? is the measured strength, \u375?\u7522? the predicted strength, \u563? the mean of the measured values, and N the number of records. The Pearson correlation coefficient measures the strength of the linear association between measured and predicted values [34]:\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab R = (\u8721?{\sub i=1}{\super N} (y{\sub i} \u8722? y\u772?)(y\u772?{\sub i} \u8722? y\u772?\u772?))/(\u8730?(\u8721?{\sub i=1}{\super N} (y{\sub i} \u8722? y\u772?){\super 2})\u8730?(\u8721?{\sub i=1}{\super N} (y\u772?{\sub i} \u8722? y\u772?\u772?){\super 2}))\tab (31)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 The coefficient of determination expresses the proportion of the variance in the measured strength that the model explains:\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab R{\super 2} = 1 \u8722? (\u8721?{\sub i=1}{\super N} (y{\sub i} \u8722? y\u772?{\sub i}){\super 2})/(\u8721?{\sub i=1}{\super N} (y{\sub i} \u8722? y\u772?){\super 2})\tab (32)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 The mean absolute error reports the average magnitude of the prediction error in the physical units of strength, giving equal weight to all errors:\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab MAE = (1)/(N)\u8721?{\sub i=1}{\super N} |y{\sub i} \u8722? y\u772?{\sub i}|\tab (33)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 The root mean square error also has units of strength but penalises large deviations more heavily because the errors are squared before averaging:\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \tab RMSE = \u8730?((1)/(N)\u8721?{\sub i=1}{\super N} (y{\sub i} \u8722? y\u772?{\sub i}){\super 2})\tab (34)\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Two further indicators complete the suite. The mean absolute percentage error (MAPE) expresses the average error as a percentage of the measured value and so allows comparison across strength levels, while the Nash\u8211?Sutcliffe efficiency (NSE) ranges from minus infinity to unity and equals one for a perfect model, zero when the model is only as good as predicting the mean, and negative values when it is worse than the mean. An ideal model simultaneously maximises R, R\u178?, and NSE toward unity while driving MAE, RMSE, and MAPE toward zero.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 No single statistic captures every aspect of predictive quality, which is why a suite of six indicators is reported. Correlation-based measures such as R and R\u178? reward models that track the pattern of the data but are insensitive to a systematic bias, whereas RMSE and MAE express the error in physical units and expose such a bias directly. RMSE weights large errors more heavily and is therefore the more demanding criterion when occasional large deviations are costly, while MAE gives a more robust picture of typical performance. MAPE places the error on a relative scale, and NSE benchmarks the model against the trivial predictor that always returns the mean. Reading the indicators together, rather than in isolation, guards against drawing an over-favourable conclusion from any one of them.\par
\pard\sb320\sa120{\b\fs28 7  Results and Discussion}\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 All eight models were trained on the normalised training subset. Their hyper-parameters were optimised by grid search coupled with ten-fold cross-validation, and the configurations that minimised the cross-validated error were retained. The selected settings are listed in Table 2.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 {\fs20 \b Table 2  }{\fs20 Principal hyper-parameters selected for each model by ten-fold cross-validated grid search.}\par
\pard
