\pard\sa120
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 The agreement between measured and predicted strength on the unseen testing records is shown graphically in Fig. 4, where each panel plots the predictions of one model against the experimental values together with the line of perfect agreement. The scatter tightens progressively from CART to XGBoost: the points for the boosted ensembles cluster closely around the diagonal across the whole strength range, whereas the single trees show visible dispersion and a tendency to over-predict low strengths and under-predict high ones, a signature of the coarse, piecewise-constant surface produced by a single partition [35].\par
\pard\qc\sa120\sl276\slmult1 {\pict\pngblip\picwgoal8640\pichgoal4952
}\par
\pard\qc\sa120\sl276\slmult1 {\fs20 \b Fig. 4  }{\fs20 Measured versus predicted compressive strength on the testing set for the eight models; the diagonal is the line of perfect prediction.}\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Figure 5 compares the models directly. Panel (a) contrasts the training and testing R\u178? values and shows that the gap between them, an indicator of overfitting, is largest for the single trees and for Extra Trees and smallest for random forest and the boosted ensembles, confirming that the regularisation built into gradient boosting controls variance effectively. Panel (b) ranks the models by testing RMSE and makes the monotone improvement from CART to XGBoost immediately visible [36].\par
\pard\qc\sa120\sl276\slmult1 {\pict\pngblip\picwgoal8640\pichgoal4846
}\par
\pard\qc\sa120\sl276\slmult1 {\fs20 \b Fig. 5  }{\fs20 Comparative performance: (a) training and testing R\u178? for each model; (b) testing root mean square error.}\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 A Taylor diagram, which condenses the correlation and the standard-deviation match into a single plot, is given in Fig. 6 for the testing stage. The point representing XGBoost lies closest to the reference marker on the horizontal axis, indicating both the highest correlation and the best reproduction of the observed variability, with GBRT and random forest only marginally behind. The single trees lie farthest from the reference, consistent with the numerical results of Table 3 [37].\par
\pard\qc\sa120\sl276\slmult1 {\pict\pngblip\picwgoal8640\pichgoal8082
}\par
\pard\qc\sa120\sl276\slmult1 {\fs20 \b Fig. 6  }{\fs20 Taylor diagram for the testing stage; the model nearest the observed reference point is the most accurate.}\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Table 4 presents the final ranking of the models together with their category. The ordering is unambiguous. Boosted ensembles take the top three places, bagged ensembles follow, and the three single trees occupy the lowest positions. The practical message is that, for recycled aggregate concrete, the extra computational cost of training a boosted ensemble is repaid by a substantial and reliable gain in accuracy [38]. When interpretability is paramount and a modest loss of accuracy is acceptable, the M5P model tree offers a transparent compromise because its leaf-level linear equations can be inspected directly [39].\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 {\fs20 \b Table 4  }{\fs20 Overall ranking of the models by testing performance.}\par
\pard
