\pard\sa120
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 The difference between training and testing accuracy deserves particular attention, because it measures how well a model generalises rather than how well it memorises. For the single trees this gap is pronounced: CART, for example, explains 90.2% of the training variance but only 86.1% of the testing variance. Extra Trees fit the training data almost perfectly yet fall back noticeably on unseen records, a reminder that a very flexible learner can still overfit. Random forest and, especially, the boosted ensembles show the smallest gaps, so their high training accuracy is matched by correspondingly high testing accuracy. This behaviour is the practical expression of the bias\u8211?variance trade-off: aggregation lowers variance without inflating bias, and the explicit regularisation of gradient boosting tightens the control further.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Accuracy is not the only consideration in model selection; training cost and ease of use also matter. The single trees and Extra Trees train in a fraction of a second on a database of this size, random forest in a few seconds, and the boosted ensembles in the order of tens of seconds once the hyper-parameter search is included. For a database of several hundred records these differences are immaterial on modern hardware, so the superior accuracy of boosting comes at negligible practical cost. Should the database grow by orders of magnitude, the efficient implementation of XGBoost, with its support for parallel and out-of-core computation, would preserve this favourable balance, whereas an exhaustive search over very deep single trees would scale less gracefully.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100{\b\fs26 7.1  Feature Importance and Sensitivity Analysis}\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 To open the best model to inspection, a SHAP (Shapley additive explanations) analysis was carried out on the XGBoost predictions [40]. SHAP assigns to each input, for every prediction, a contribution derived from cooperative game theory, and the mean absolute contribution across the dataset provides a consistent global measure of importance. The resulting ranking is shown in Fig. 7.\par
\pard\qc\sa120\sl276\slmult1 {\pict\pngblip\picwgoal8640\pichgoal5229
}\par
\pard\qc\sa120\sl276\slmult1 {\fs20 \b Fig. 7  }{\fs20 Mean absolute SHAP contribution of each predictor for the XGBoost model, ranked from most to least influential.}\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Curing age is by far the most influential variable, which is expected because strength development follows the progress of cement hydration over time [41]. The water-to-cement ratio ranks second, in keeping with Abrams' classical law that lower ratios yield denser, stronger pastes. Cement content is third, and the recycled-aggregate replacement ratio is a strong fourth, confirming quantitatively that increasing the proportion of recycled aggregate tends to lower strength, chiefly through the porous adhered mortar and the additional weak interfacial zone [42]. The water absorption of the recycled aggregate and the superplasticiser dosage have moderate influence, while the contents of natural and fine aggregate are the least important, since within a fixed mixing procedure their variation is largely compensated by the other proportions. These attributions are physically coherent and give engineers a clear, evidence-based order of priority for controlling the strength of recycled aggregate concrete [43].\par
\pard\sb200\sa100{\b\fs26 7.2  Comparison with Previous Studies}\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 The ranking obtained here is consistent with the broader literature on data-driven strength prediction for cementitious materials. Studies that pitted a single neural network or a single tree against bagged or boosted ensembles have repeatedly found the ensembles to be more accurate and more stable, and several have singled out gradient boosting and random forest as the leading performers on tabular concrete data. The testing coefficient of determination obtained for XGBoost, close to 0.96, is in the same high band reported for well-tuned ensembles on comparable recycled-aggregate and high-performance concrete datasets, which lends external credibility to the present results while the common pipeline makes the internal comparison strictly controlled.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 At the same time, the study clarifies a point that is sometimes blurred when algorithms are compared across different datasets: the advantage of boosting over bagging, though real, is smaller than the advantage of any ensemble over a single tree. In other words, the first and largest gain comes from aggregating trees at all; the refinement from bagging to boosting then adds a further, more modest improvement. Recognising the relative size of these two steps helps practitioners decide how much modelling sophistication a given application actually warrants.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100{\b\fs26 7.3  Limitations and Practical Implications}\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Several limitations temper the conclusions. The models are statistical interpolators trained on a finite database, so their predictions are reliable only within the range of the variables they have seen and should not be extrapolated to exotic mixes or unusual aggregates. The database, although diverse, inevitably reflects the materials, curing regimes, and test protocols of its source studies, and systematic differences between laboratories contribute an irreducible scatter that bounds the accuracy any model can reach. The present work also addresses compressive strength alone, whereas the structural acceptability of recycled aggregate concrete depends additionally on tensile behaviour, stiffness, shrinkage, and durability.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Notwithstanding these limitations, the practical implications are clear. A tuned gradient boosting model can serve as a rapid pre-screening tool that narrows a wide design space to a handful of promising mixes before any specimens are cast, saving material, time, and cost. The feature-importance ranking tells the mix designer which variables to control most tightly, and the transparent M5P equations provide an auditable fall-back when a regulator or client requires an explicit formula. Embedding such models in mix-design software would make the confident, performance-based use of recycled aggregate considerably more accessible to practising engineers.\par
\pard\sb320\sa120{\b\fs28 8  Conclusions}\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 This study compared three single tree-based learners and five ensemble learners for predicting the compressive strength of recycled aggregate concrete, using a unified database of 514 records, an identical preprocessing pipeline, and six evaluation metrics. The main conclusions are as follows.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 (1) Ensemble learning delivers a clear and consistent accuracy advantage over single trees. Every ensemble outperformed every single tree on all metrics, demonstrating that aggregating many base learners controls the high variance of an individual regression tree.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 (2) Boosted ensembles are the most accurate. XGBoost achieved the best generalisation (testing R\u178? = 0.962, RMSE = 3.41 MPa, MAE = 2.58 MPa, MAPE = 7.2%, NSE = 0.961), closely followed by gradient boosted regression trees and random forest. The single CART tree was the weakest model, with a testing R\u178? of 0.861.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 (3) The regularised formulation of gradient boosting also produced the smallest gap between training and testing accuracy, indicating strong resistance to overfitting, whereas the single trees and, to a lesser degree, Extra Trees showed the largest gaps.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 (4) The SHAP analysis identified curing age, the water-to-cement ratio, cement content, and the recycled-aggregate replacement ratio as the dominant predictors. These attributions are consistent with the known mechanics of concrete and give practitioners a ranked set of variables to control when targeting a specified strength.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 (5) For routine mix design, a tuned XGBoost or gradient boosting model is recommended when accuracy is the priority, while the M5P model tree offers a transparent and reasonably accurate alternative when an explicit, inspectable equation is required.\par
\pard\qj\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Future work should enlarge and diversify the database to include more field data and additional treatments of recycled aggregate, extend the comparison to hybrid and stacked ensembles and to deep-learning models, and incorporate durability and long-term performance indicators alongside compressive strength so that the sustainable use of recycled aggregate can be assessed more comprehensively [44].\par
